<<~ ahu #/head>>

! LarVessel ~ one class, four hosts, no subclass

A browser tab, a Node process, a worker and a device all open the SAME vessel class. The Repo's
storage and network adapters carry the environmental difference, and a `capabilities` preset names
what the host can physically do. Nothing about a host earns it a different type.

''Relay and browser vessels participate symmetrically.'' Neither one holds content truth.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! The invariants

# ''Capability declares a SURFACE, never authority.'' `LarVesselCapabilities` answers //what can this
host physically do// — reach a disk, hop CORS, hold a persistent relay, open a BroadcastChannel, write
a non-idempotent local effect. It answers nothing about permission.
# ''Receipt law decides a write, and process role never does.'' A vessel advertising
`hostfulReactions` still owes an authority receipt and an idempotency key before any effect lands. A
host that CAN write holds no standing to write.
# ''The default grants nothing.'' Construction starts from `LAR_VESSEL_CAPABILITIES_NONE` and layers
the caller's partial over it, so a capability arrives only where someone named it. The three presets
— NONE, NODE, BROWSER — state a host's reach in one place rather than scattering host checks through
the runtime.
# ''A vessel carries a name or refuses to open.'' Absent `vesselId` the constructor throws; a vessel
with no id could neither derive a stable actor nor answer for its own effects.
# ''The factory owns the handles; the vessel receives an interface.'' `store` arrives as an assembled
`LarTiddlerStore` over the full composite stack (system -> corpus:* -> wiki -> draft), with the
factories having built the CompositeStore and marked sync complete on the writable layers. The vessel
holds no Automerge DocHandle of its own.
# ''Readiness binds to the VM pool alone.'' A vessel reads `ready` once a pool attaches and returns to
unready on `dispose`. Nothing else moves that answer.
# ''A projection subscribes to the WHOLE stack.'' `addProjection` returns the unsubscribe, and a
projection sees composite changes rather than one layer's.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-identity-slot>>

!! What the identity slot admits today

The vessel takes an `IdentitySlot` or carries NONE. `identity` reads `IdentitySlot | null` and the
constructor binds `opts.identity ?? null`, so ''no default slot stands'' — a vessel that names none
grants nothing through one, and the alpha line keeps no alias in the empty seat.

''The admission gates stand elsewhere, and the asymmetry holds by design.'' The node `sharePolicy`
gates inbound network peers at the daemon-auth gate; the browser admits only same-origin and
in-process peers. A browser vessel reaches no peer a node vessel would have had to authenticate.

''The verdict a slot doc owes belongs to the PersonaGroup ring.'' `makePersonaGroupIdentityRing`
(`packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-group-ring.ts`) reads the asking face's own grant records,
fail-closes where the peer proved no vessel key, carries no field for a realm's `keptBy` and none for
a roster, and `compose` widens a base gate while narrowing nothing. It stands ''BUILT AND UNWIRED'':
its definition, the barrel export and its own test hold every reference to it, and
`open-browser-vessel.ts` passes `identityRing = null`. The wiring waits at the two sharePolicy shores
— the node's shore holds a repo and a catalog url while the PersonaGroup plane and its grant records
rest in the daemon island's store, so it waits on one message carrying that plane across; the browser's
shore moves nothing until it admits a relay peer at all, where the ring's own-hand class
short-circuits. A reader who takes the ring for LIVE repeats the same fault in the other direction.

''Keyhive slots in without moving this interface.'' `KeyhiveSlot` stays typed to four calls —
capability verification, member add and remove, application-secret derivation — so the three-layer
stack (convergent capabilities, Group CRDT, BeeKEM) lands behind the same surface the vessel already
names.

<<~/ahu>>

